  "response": "## The test I'd strengthen first: `crime_00065`\n\n```\n\"file\": \"test/index.test.ts\",\n\"lines\": [537, 548],\n\"discriminator\": \"native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly\"\n```\n\nIts evidence, verbatim:\n\n> - `test: \"native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly\"`\n> - `0 expect/assert calls`\n> - `lines 537-548`\n\nand the summary:\n\n> `Test \"native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly\" contains no expect/assert calls. A test that only runs setup gives agents false confidence.`\n\nOf the eleven `weak_test_signal` findings with `0 expect/assert calls`, this is the **only one whose subject is an esbuild plugin** — the others cover `--dts` bundling (`test/example.test.ts`, `test/graphql.test.ts`) and tsconfig plumbing (`test/tsconfig.test.ts`). It matches the reported symptom exactly: an esbuild-plugin regression that CI stays green through.\n\nI confirmed the mechanism in the fixture. The test body is a bare `await run(...)` with nothing after it (`test/index.test.ts:537-548`), and `run()` in `test/utils.ts:52-57` throws only on a non-zero exit code. So the test asserts precisely one thing: *tsup didn't crash*. Every observable output of `nativeNodeModulesPlugin` — which branch resolved, which namespace the module landed in, what got emitted — is unchecked.\n\n## The assertion I'd add\n\n`src/esbuild/native-node-module.ts:11-24` has two exit branches for a `.node` import, and the whole point of this test's `hi.node.js` fixture is that it hits the *second* one — `require.resolve` returns a path ending in `.js`, not `.node`, so the module must stay in the `file` namespace and be inlined, **not** get routed through the `node-file` virtual namespace whose `onLoad` wraps it in `try { module.exports = require(path) } catch {}` (lines 30-34). Nothing currently checks which branch ran.\n\n```ts\ntest('native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly', async () => {\n  const { output, outFiles } = await run(\n    getTestName(),\n    {\n      'input.tsx': `export * from './hi.node'`,\n      'hi.node.js': `export const hi = 'hi'`,\n    },\n    {\n      entry: ['input.tsx'],\n    },\n  )\n\n  // `hi.node.js` is not a real native addon, so the plugin must take the plain\n  // `{ path: resolvedId }` branch and let esbuild inline it...\n  expect(output).toContain(`hi = \"hi\"`)\n  // ...not route it through the `node-file` namespace, whose onLoad emits a\n  // runtime `require()` wrapper and copies the addon out as a file asset.\n  expect(output).not.toContain('module.exports = require(path)')\n  expect(outFiles).toEqual(['input.js'])\n})\n```\n\n`expect(output).not.toContain('module.exports = require(path)')` is the load-bearing line — it's the one assertion that distinguishes the two branches at lines 15-23, which is exactly where a regression in this plugin lands. `outFiles` catches the other failure mode: if the module were misrouted, esbuild's `file` loader (`opts.loader['.node'] = 'file'`, line 54) would emit a copied `hi-<hash>.node` alongside `input.js`.\n\nRunner-up: `crime_00068`, `test/tsconfig.test.ts` \"custom tsconfig\", `0 expect/assert calls`, `lines 4-17` — same shape (bare `run()`, `--tsconfig tsconfig.build.json` passed and nothing verified), but it isn't plugin-related, so it doesn't explain this regression.\n\n## One caveat on the scan output\n\nFour of the `0 expect/assert calls` findings look like detector false positives, and I'd ignore them rather than \"fix\" the tests:\n\n- `crime_00066` / `crime_00067` — `test/shims.test.ts`, reported at `lines 5-5` and `lines 69-72`\n- `crime_00059` / `crime_00060` — `test/experimental-dts.test.ts`, reported at `lines 319-322` and `lines 392-392`\n\nThose tests all use vitest's destructured context signature — `async ({ expect, task }) => {` — and do assert (`test/shims.test.ts:49` and `:65`, for instance). The collapsed line ranges suggest the detector loses the function body when the callback destructures its parameter, then reports zero assertions. The six true positives in `test/example.test.ts`, `test/graphql.test.ts`, `test/tsconfig.test.ts`, and `test/index.test.ts` all use the plain `async () =>` form, which the detector handles correctly.",
